%p You have three options for creating and storing a passkey:

.d-flex.flex-row.gap-2.mb-3
  .d
    = icon("usb-drive", size: 24)
  .d
    %b Security Key:
    You can store passkeys on a physical device called a "security key" (or sometimes called a "hardware key"). This is the most secure method,
    but is not backed up if you lose the physical device (you can add multiple passkeys).
    We recommend the YubiKey 5C NFC ($30).

.d-flex.flex-row.gap-2.mb-3
  .d
    = icon("window-sidebar", size: 24)
  .d
    %b Password Manager:
    Password managers such as Bitwarden, 1Password, and NordPass can store passkeys,
    back them up, and make them available across all your devices. You must install the
    browser extension for these password managers in order to log in with a passkey.

.d-flex.flex-row.gap-2.mb-3
  .d
    = icon("pc-display-horizontal", size: 24)
  .d
    %b Operating System:
    .mb-2
      Most operating systems will store a passkey, although this locks you into only using that operating system.
      This method is not recommended, but possibly this is already enabled for your device and you can start
      using passkeys right away.
    .d-flex.flex-row.gap-2.mb-2
      .d
        = icon("apple")
      .d
        macOS & iOS 16+: Passkeys may be stored in your iCloud account using Apple Passwords. For macOS,
        you can alternately store passkeys in the Keychain without an iCloud account, but then the
        passkeys are not backed up.
    .d-flex.flex-row.gap-2.mb-2
      .d
        = icon("windows")
      .d
        Windows 11: Passkeys may be stored by Windows 11 when Windows Hello is enabled. These passkeys are not
        backed up or usable on other devices (and may be insecure depending on how Windows Hello is configured).
        Passkeys stored with Windows Hello do not support automatic encryption, so you will need to manually
        specify an encryption phrase.
    .d-flex.flex-row.gap-2.mb-2
      .d
        = icon("android2")
      .d
        Android: Passkeys may be stored in the Google Password Manager if you have a Google account.
%p
  Passkeys are not portable: they cannot be moved from one storage mechanism to another. However, you can add
  and remove passkeys from different sources under your <b>Authentication</b> settings of this website.
%p
  In your settings, you may also specify an optional recovery code, which will allow you to add a new passkey if you
  have lost your existing ones.

